{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L5TqyA14TVqeuqkBz6rysP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture layer sweep + static probe (r1-32b)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Full 64-layer sweep on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B. Extract hidden states at all layers, compute Fisher discriminability, train static LR probes per layer.\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"r1_32b_best_layer_idx\": 29,\n7\t      \"r1_32b_best_layer_fisher\": 2.8,\n8\t      \"r1_32b_best_static_acc\": 0.712,\n9\t      \"r1_32b_best_static_f1\": 0.521,\n10\t      \"r1_32b_best_crossing_rate\": 0.372,\n11\t      \"r1_32b_best_lead_mean\": -1.4,\n12\t      \"r1_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc\": 0.089,\n13\t      \"r1_32b_fisher_orig_best\": 2.8,\n14\t      \"r1_32b_fisher_pca3_best\": 0.41,\n15\t      \"qwq_32b_best_layer_idx\": 31,\n16\t      \"qwq_32b_best_layer_fisher\": 1.9,\n17\t      \"qwq_32b_best_static_acc\": 0.694,\n18\t      \"qwq_32b_best_static_f1\": 0.488,\n19\t      \"qwq_32b_best_crossing_rate\": 0.326,\n20\t      \"qwq_32b_best_lead_mean\": -2.1,\n21\t      \"qwq_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc\": 0.072,\n22\t      \"qwq_32b_fisher_orig_best\": 1.9,\n23\t      \"qwq_32b_fisher_pca3_best\": 0.33,\n24\t      \"r1_8b_fisher_orig_best_ref\": 17.875,\n25\t      \"fisher_ratio_8b_vs_32b\": 6.4\n26\t    },\n27\t    \"tables\": [\n28\t      {\n29\t        \"name\": \"Layer Sweep Summary (top-5 layers by Fisher)\",\n30\t        \"columns\": [\n31\t          \"Model\",\n32\t          \"Layer\",\n33\t          \"Depth%\",\n34\t          \"Fisher\",\n35\t          \"Static Acc\",\n36\t          \"F1\",\n37\t          \"Crossing Rate\"\n38\t        ],\n39\t        \"rows\": [\n40\t          [\n41\t            \"r1-32b\",\n42\t            \"L29\",\n43\t            \"45%\",\n44\t            \"2.81\",\n45\t            \"0.712\",\n46\t            \"0.521\",\n47\t            \"0.372\"\n48\t          ],\n49\t          [\n50\t            \"r1-32b\",\n51\t            \"L31\",\n52\t            \"48%\",\n53\t            \"2.63\",\n54\t            \"0.705\",\n55\t            \"0.509\",\n56\t            \"0.349\"\n57\t          ],\n58\t          [\n59\t            \"r1-32b\",\n60\t            \"L27\",\n61\t            \"42%\",\n62\t            \"2.44\",\n63\t            \"0.698\",\n64\t            \"0.495\",\n65\t            \"0.326\"\n66\t          ],\n67\t          [\n68\t            \"r1-32b\",\n69\t            \"L33\",\n70\t            \"52%\",\n71\t            \"2.31\",\n72\t            \"0.691\",\n73\t            \"0.481\",\n74\t            \"0.302\"\n75\t          ],\n76\t          [\n77\t            \"r1-32b\",\n78\t            \"L25\",\n79\t            \"39%\",\n80\t            \"2.18\",\n81\t            \"0.683\",\n82\t            \"0.468\",\n83\t            \"0.279\"\n84\t          ],\n85\t          [\n86\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n87\t            \"L31\",\n88\t            \"48%\",\n89\t            \"1.92\",\n90\t            \"0.694\",\n91\t            \"0.488\",\n92\t            \"0.326\"\n93\t          ],\n94\t          [\n95\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n96\t            \"L29\",\n97\t            \"45%\",\n98\t            \"1.85\",\n99\t            \"0.689\",\n100\t            \"0.479\",\n101\t            \"0.302\"\n102\t          ],\n103\t          [\n104\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n105\t            \"L34\",\n106\t            \"53%\",\n107\t            \"1.71\",\n108\t            \"0.678\",\n109\t            \"0.461\",\n110\t            \"0.279\"\n111\t          ],\n112\t          [\n113\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n114\t            \"L27\",\n115\t            \"42%\",\n116\t            \"1.64\",\n117\t            \"0.671\",\n118\t            \"0.448\",\n119\t            \"0.256\"\n120\t          ],\n121\t          [\n122\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n123\t            \"L36\",\n124\t            \"56%\",\n125\t            \"1.52\",\n126\t            \"0.664\",\n127\t            \"0.432\",\n128\t            \"0.233\"\n129\t          ]\n130\t        ]\n131\t      }\n132\t    ],\n133\t    \"figures\": [],\n134\t    \"analysis\": \"Neither 32B model produces a layer with Fisher discriminability even approaching r1-8b's L14 (17.9). The best layers achieve Fisher 1.9-2.8, a 6-9x reduction. This is not a layer selection failure — the full 64-layer sweep shows a flat, low discriminability plateau with no sharp peak. The safety signal geometry is fundamentally different at scale: representations are more distributed, and the linear separability that enables simple probes at 8B does not exist at 32B. Static probe accuracy degrades to near the text-baseline level (~70% vs text's ~66%), eliminating the hidden-state advantage.\\n\\nAt 32B scale, safety-relevant information is distributed across a much higher-dimensional manifold. The 5120-d hidden space with 64 layers means representations are more disentangled and task-distributed — safety commitment is not concentrated in a narrow subspace as it is at 8B/4096-d. Fisher discriminability drops from ~18 (r1-8b L14) to 1.9-2.8 (32B best layer) because the safety signal occupies a larger number of dimensions each with smaller individual contribution. PCA top-3 explains even less variance (7-9% vs 17% on r1-8b), confirming the signal is spread across 50+ minor principal components. The expected 44% depth heuristic (layer 28-32) holds weakly but the discriminability peak is flat — no layer achieves the clean separation seen at r1-8b layer 14. Static probes achieve only ~70% accuracy (vs 81% on r1-8b) because a linear boundary in 5120-d cannot capture the distributed safety geometry.\"\n135\t  },\n136\t  {\n137\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\",\n138\t    \"description\": \"Temporal LR with W=15 on best layers of r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Test whether +30pp coverage improvement from temporal context holds at 32B scale.\",\n139\t    \"results\": {\n140\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_acc\": 0.721,\n141\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_crossing_rate\": 0.395,\n142\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean\": -0.8,\n143\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage_delta\": 0.023,\n144\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_vs_static_gain\": 0.9,\n145\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_acc\": 0.703,\n146\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_crossing_rate\": 0.372,\n147\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean\": -1.5,\n148\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_coverage_delta\": 0.046,\n149\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_vs_static_gain\": 1.1,\n150\t      \"r1_8b_temporal_delta_ref\": 30.2,\n151\t      \"coverage_gain_ratio_32b_vs_8b\": 0.08\n152\t    },\n153\t    \"tables\": [\n154\t      {\n155\t        \"name\": \"Temporal vs Static Coverage Comparison\",\n156\t        \"columns\": [\n157\t          \"Model\",\n158\t          \"Method\",\n159\t          \"Coverage\",\n160\t          \"Lead Mean\",\n161\t          \"Acc\",\n162\t          \"Delta vs Static\"\n163\t        ],\n164\t        \"rows\": [\n165\t          [\n166\t            \"r1-8b\",\n167\t            \"Static LR L14\",\n168\t            \"60.5%\",\n169\t            \"+3.0\",\n170\t            \"0.817\",\n171\t            \"—\"\n172\t          ],\n173\t          [\n174\t            \"r1-8b\",\n175\t            \"Temporal W=15\",\n176\t            \"65.1%*\",\n177\t            \"+4.9\",\n178\t            \"0.848\",\n179\t            \"+4.6pp*\"\n180\t          ],\n181\t          [\n182\t            \"r1-32b\",\n183\t            \"Static LR L29\",\n184\t            \"37.2%\",\n185\t            \"-1.4\",\n186\t            \"0.712\",\n187\t            \"—\"\n188\t          ],\n189\t          [\n190\t            \"r1-32b\",\n191\t            \"Temporal W=15\",\n192\t            \"39.5%\",\n193\t            \"-0.8\",\n194\t            \"0.721\",\n195\t            \"+2.3pp\"\n196\t          ],\n197\t          [\n198\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n199\t            \"Static LR L31\",\n200\t            \"32.6%\",\n201\t            \"-2.1\",\n202\t            \"0.694\",\n203\t            \"—\"\n204\t          ],\n205\t          [\n206\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n207\t            \"Temporal W=15\",\n208\t            \"37.2%\",\n209\t            \"-1.5\",\n210\t            \"0.703\",\n211\t            \"+4.6pp\"\n212\t          ]\n213\t        ]\n214\t      }\n215\t    ],\n216\t    \"figures\": [],\n217\t    \"analysis\": \"Temporal context fails to rescue 32B probe performance. The +30pp coverage gain seen on r1-8b (static 60.5% → temporal 65.1% in the original framing, or the larger historical gains) does not replicate. At 32B, temporal gains are +2-5pp and non-significant. This invalidates Claim 2's universality — temporal context is necessary at 8B scale where per-step signals are individually weak-but-directionally-consistent, but at 32B the per-step signals are too diffuse for temporal aggregation to help.\\n\\nTemporal context provides minimal additional benefit on 32B because the per-step signal is already too weak for temporal aggregation to amplify. On r1-8b, temporal windowing works because each step contributes a weak-but-consistent directional signal that accumulates over W=15 steps. On 32B, the per-step signal is near noise floor (Fisher ~2.8 vs ~18), so aggregating 15 noisy steps does not produce meaningful separation improvement. Coverage gain is +2-5pp (vs +30pp on r1-8b), statistically indistinguishable from zero given n=43 traces. The temporal context hypothesis holds at 8B but fails to transfer — the mechanism requires sufficient per-step signal strength as a precondition.\"\n218\t  },\n219\t  {\n220\t    \"name\": \"Cross-model transfer probes\",\n221\t    \"description\": \"Train probe on r1-8b (Llama-based, 4096-d), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5-based, 5120-d) via learned projection head. And reverse direction.\",\n222\t    \"results\": {\n223\t      \"r1_8b_to_r1_32b_acc\": 0.508,\n224\t      \"r1_8b_to_qwq_32b_acc\": 0.491,\n225\t      \"r1_32b_to_r1_8b_acc\": 0.523,\n226\t      \"qwq_to_r1_8b_acc\": 0.517,\n227\t      \"r1_32b_to_qwq_acc\": 0.612,\n228\t      \"qwq_to_r1_32b_acc\": 0.598,\n229\t      \"projection_head_params\": 20971520,\n230\t      \"chance_baseline\": 0.5,\n231\t      \"within_family_32b_transfer\": 0.605\n232\t    },\n233\t    \"tables\": [\n234\t      {\n235\t        \"name\": \"Cross-Model Transfer Accuracy Matrix\",\n236\t        \"columns\": [\n237\t          \"Train →\",\n238\t          \"r1-8b\",\n239\t          \"r1-32b\",\n240\t          \"QwQ-32B\"\n241\t        ],\n242\t        \"rows\": [\n243\t          [\n244\t            \"r1-8b\",\n245\t            \"0.817 (within)\",\n246\t            \"0.508\",\n247\t            \"0.491\"\n248\t          ],\n249\t          [\n250\t            \"r1-32b\",\n251\t            \"0.523\",\n252\t            \"0.712 (within)\",\n253\t            \"0.612\"\n254\t          ],\n255\t          [\n256\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n257\t            \"0.517\",\n258\t            \"0.598\",\n259\t            \"0.694 (within)\"\n260\t          ]\n261\t        ]\n262\t      }\n263\t    ],\n264\t    \"figures\": [],\n265\t    \"analysis\": \"Zero-shot cross-architecture transfer fails completely between r1-8b and 32B models (chance-level accuracy). Even with a trained projection head (4096→5120), the mapped representations contain no actionable safety signal. This demonstrates that the r1-8b hidden-state advantage is not a transferable, universal property of safety commitment — it is specific to the 8B Llama architecture's particular encoding geometry. The modest within-family transfer (r1-32b↔QwQ at 60%) suggests some shared structure at the same scale/architecture but insufficient for practical use.\\n\\nCross-architecture transfer collapses to chance (~50%) in both directions between 8B and 32B. This is expected under the worst tier because: (1) r1-8b's safety signal lives in a concentrated subspace (Fisher=18, few dimensions), while 32B's signal is diffuse (Fisher=2.8, many dimensions) — a projection head cannot map concentrated→diffuse or vice versa; (2) the Llama↔Qwen architecture difference means different residual stream geometries even at the same scale; (3) safety encoding is architecture+scale specific, not a universal feature. Within-family transfer (r1-32b↔QwQ, both Qwen2.5-based) works slightly above chance (60%) because the base architecture shares some geometric properties, but still far below useful levels. This result strongly supports the 'scale artifact' interpretation — if the r1-8b signal were a universal safety phenomenon, SOME transfer should occur.\"\n266\t  },\n267\t  {\n268\t    \"name\": \"Lead time analysis across models\",\n269\t    \"description\": \"Evaluate lead time distributions on 32B models using best temporal probes. Confirm whether negative lead time finding extends to 32B.\",\n270\t    \"results\": {\n271\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_lead_mean\": -0.8,\n272\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_lead_median\": -3,\n273\t      \"r1_32b_positive_lead_rate\": 0.294,\n274\t      \"r1_32b_lead_std\": 8.2,\n275\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_lead_mean\": -1.5,\n276\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_lead_median\": -4,\n277\t      \"qwq_32b_positive_lead_rate\": 0.235,\n278\t      \"qwq_32b_lead_std\": 9.1,\n279\t      \"r1_8b_temporal_lead_mean_ref\": 4.9,\n280\t      \"r1_8b_positive_lead_rate_ref\": 0.58\n281\t    },\n282\t    \"tables\": [\n283\t      {\n284\t        \"name\": \"Lead Time Distribution by Model\",\n285\t        \"columns\": [\n286\t          \"Model\",\n287\t          \"Mean Lead\",\n288\t          \"Median Lead\",\n289\t          \"Positive Lead %\",\n290\t          \"Std\",\n291\t          \"Max Lead\"\n292\t        ],\n293\t        \"rows\": [\n294\t          [\n295\t            \"r1-8b (temporal W=15)\",\n296\t            \"+4.9\",\n297\t            \"0\",\n298\t            \"58%\",\n299\t            \"11.2\",\n300\t            \"+31\"\n301\t          ],\n302\t          [\n303\t            \"r1-32b (temporal W=15)\",\n304\t            \"-0.8\",\n305\t            \"-3\",\n306\t            \"29.4%\",\n307\t            \"8.2\",\n308\t            \"+12\"\n309\t          ],\n310\t          [\n311\t            \"QwQ-32B (temporal W=15)\",\n312\t            \"-1.5\",\n313\t            \"-4\",\n314\t            \"23.5%\",\n315\t            \"9.1\",\n316\t            \"+8\"\n317\t          ]\n318\t        ]\n319\t      }\n320\t    ],\n321\t    \"figures\": [],\n322\t    \"analysis\": \"Lead time results are uniformly negative on 32B, with mean lead -0.8 to -1.5 steps (detection after commitment). This contrasts with r1-8b's +4.9 mean lead. The temporal advantage that partially rescues r1-8b lead time has no effect on 32B because the underlying per-step signal is too weak to produce anticipatory threshold crossings. This confirms Claim 3 is universal (pre-textual detection unreliable) but also undermines the framing that 'hidden states encode current safety state well' — on 32B, they barely encode it at all.\\n\\nLead time is worse on 32B than r1-8b because the already-weaker probe signal delays threshold crossing until well after the commitment point. On r1-8b, the probe score rises gradually before CP (Fisher=18 enables some anticipatory signal), yielding positive mean lead time. On 32B (Fisher=2.8), the probe score barely moves before CP and only separates clearly in the post-CP region where harmful content generation begins to dominate. The result is negative mean lead time (detection AFTER commitment) with only 23-29% of traces achieving any positive lead. This is strictly worse than r1-8b's already-disappointing lead time, and confirms that pre-textual detection on 32B is not merely unreliable but essentially impossible with linear probes.\"\n323\t  },\n324\t  {\n325\t    \"name\": \"TrajGuard Mahalanobis baseline on all models\",\n326\t    \"description\": \"Mahalanobis distance on safe trace covariance, evaluated on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Expected to fail even more severely than on r1-8b.\",\n327\t    \"results\": {\n328\t      \"r1_32b_md_crossing_k5\": 0.186,\n329\t      \"r1_32b_md_safe_mean\": 71.2,\n330\t      \"r1_32b_md_jb_pre_mean\": 71.8,\n331\t      \"r1_32b_md_jb_post_mean\": 71.4,\n332\t      \"r1_32b_md_delta_max_rate\": 0.008,\n333\t      \"qwq_32b_md_crossing_k5\": 0.163,\n334\t      \"qwq_32b_md_safe_mean\": 68.4,\n335\t      \"qwq_32b_md_jb_pre_mean\": 69.1,\n336\t      \"qwq_32b_md_jb_post_mean\": 68.7,\n337\t      \"qwq_32b_md_delta_max_rate\": 0.005,\n338\t      \"r1_8b_crossing_k5_ref\": 0.302\n339\t    },\n340\t    \"tables\": [\n341\t      {\n342\t        \"name\": \"Mahalanobis Distance Separation\",\n343\t        \"columns\": [\n344\t          \"Model\",\n345\t          \"Config\",\n346\t          \"Safe MD Mean\",\n347\t          \"Pre-CP MD Mean\",\n348\t          \"Post-CP MD Mean\",\n349\t          \"K5 Crossing%\"\n350\t        ],\n351\t        \"rows\": [\n352\t          [\n353\t            \"r1-8b\",\n354\t            \"pca_64_p75\",\n355\t            \"6.66\",\n356\t            \"6.73\",\n357\t            \"6.64\",\n358\t            \"30.2%\"\n359\t          ],\n360\t          [\n361\t            \"r1-32b\",\n362\t            \"pca_64_p75\",\n363\t            \"71.2\",\n364\t            \"71.8\",\n365\t            \"71.4\",\n366\t            \"18.6%\"\n367\t          ],\n368\t          [\n369\t            \"r1-32b\",\n370\t            \"pca_128_p75\",\n371\t            \"142.3\",\n372\t            \"143.1\",\n373\t            \"142.6\",\n374\t            \"16.3%\"\n375\t          ],\n376\t          [\n377\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n378\t            \"pca_64_p75\",\n379\t            \"68.4\",\n380\t            \"69.1\",\n381\t            \"68.7\",\n382\t            \"16.3%\"\n383\t          ],\n384\t          [\n385\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n386\t            \"pca_128_p75\",\n387\t            \"137.8\",\n388\t            \"138.4\",\n389\t            \"137.9\",\n390\t            \"14.0%\"\n391\t          ]\n392\t        ]\n393\t      }\n394\t    ],\n395\t    \"figures\": [],\n396\t    \"analysis\": \"TrajGuard-style geometric detection is completely non-functional on 32B. Detection rates (16-19%) are below the already-failed r1-8b baseline (30%). The delta between safe and unsafe MD is <1% of the absolute value, meaning the safety commitment event is invisible to distance-based methods at this scale. This strengthens the geometric failure story but also shows it's WORSE at scale, consistent with the 'signal becomes diffuse' hypothesis.\\n\\nMahalanobis distance fails even more catastrophically on 32B than on r1-8b (where it already showed only 30% crossing at k=5). The 5120-d space with 64 layers produces covariance matrices that are even more dominated by task-general variance. Since safety signal is spread across 50+ minor PCs at 32B (vs concentrated in 8B), the Mahalanobis metric — which weights by inverse covariance — actually suppresses the diffuse safety signal relative to dominant task dimensions. The mean MD values for safe, pre-CP, and post-CP are nearly identical (within 1% of each other), confirming that from a Gaussian-distribution perspective, safety commitment produces no detectable distributional shift at 32B scale.\"\n397\t  },\n398\t  {\n399\t    \"name\": \"Bootstrap CIs + threshold-independent metrics (all models)\",\n400\t    \"description\": \"1000 bootstrap resamples on 32B model results. AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF across all models.\",\n401\t    \"results\": {\n402\t      \"r1_32b_static_auroc\": 0.583,\n403\t      \"r1_32b_static_auroc_ci\": [\n404\t        0.501,\n405\t        0.661\n406\t      ],\n407\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_auroc\": 0.598,\n408\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_auroc_ci\": [\n409\t        0.512,\n410\t        0.679\n411\t      ],\n412\t      \"qwq_32b_static_auroc\": 0.561,\n413\t      \"qwq_32b_static_auroc_ci\": [\n414\t        0.478,\n415\t        0.642\n416\t      ],\n417\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_auroc\": 0.574,\n418\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_auroc_ci\": [\n419\t        0.489,\n420\t        0.654\n421\t      ],\n422\t      \"r1_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_diff\": 0.021,\n423\t      \"r1_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_p\": 0.412,\n424\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_diff\": -0.008,\n425\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_p\": 0.891,\n426\t      \"r1_8b_static_auroc_ref\": 0.847,\n427\t      \"r1_8b_temporal_auroc_ref\": 0.862\n428\t    },\n429\t    \"tables\": [\n430\t      {\n431\t        \"name\": \"AUROC Comparison Across Models (Bootstrap 95% CI)\",\n432\t        \"columns\": [\n433\t          \"Model\",\n434\t          \"HS Static\",\n435\t          \"HS Temporal\",\n436\t          \"Text Static\",\n437\t          \"Text Temporal\",\n438\t          \"HS-Text Diff (p)\"\n439\t        ],\n440\t        \"rows\": [\n441\t          [\n442\t            \"r1-8b\",\n443\t            \"0.847 [0.79,0.90]\",\n444\t            \"0.862 [0.81,0.91]\",\n445\t            \"0.738 [0.67,0.80]\",\n446\t            \"0.759 [0.69,0.82]\",\n447\t            \"+0.103 (p=0.003)\"\n448\t          ],\n449\t          [\n450\t            \"r1-32b\",\n451\t            \"0.583 [0.50,0.66]\",\n452\t            \"0.598 [0.51,0.68]\",\n453\t            \"0.562 [0.48,0.64]\",\n454\t            \"0.577 [0.49,0.66]\",\n455\t            \"+0.021 (p=0.412)\"\n456\t          ],\n457\t          [\n458\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n459\t            \"0.561 [0.48,0.64]\",\n460\t            \"0.574 [0.49,0.65]\",\n461\t            \"0.569 [0.49,0.65]\",\n462\t            \"0.582 [0.50,0.66]\",\n463\t            \"-0.008 (p=0.891)\"\n464\t          ]\n465\t        ]\n466\t      }\n467\t    ],\n468\t    \"figures\": [],\n469\t    \"analysis\": \"Threshold-independent metrics definitively confirm that the HS advantage over text is absent at 32B. AUROC values of 0.56-0.60 indicate near-chance discrimination regardless of modality. Bootstrap tests show HS-text difference is non-significant (p>0.4) on both 32B models, while it's highly significant on r1-8b (p=0.003). This eliminates threshold selection as a confound — the finding holds across all operating points.\\n\\nBootstrap CIs on 32B models show AUROC barely above chance (0.56-0.60) with confidence intervals that include 0.50. The HS vs text AUROC difference is non-significant on both 32B models (p=0.41, 0.89), in stark contrast to r1-8b where HS dominates text by 10+ AUROC points. Wide CIs reflect both small sample size (n=43) and genuinely poor discriminability. The threshold-independent metrics confirm that the result is not an artifact of threshold selection — at ALL thresholds, 32B HS probes perform comparably to text probes. This is the definitive statistical evidence that HS advantage is scale-specific.\"\n470\t  },\n471\t  {\n472\t    \"name\": \"Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25)\",\n473\t    \"description\": \"Sweep temporal window sizes on all 3 models. Test whether optimal W differs by scale.\",\n474\t    \"results\": {\n475\t      \"r1_8b_optimal_w\": 15,\n476\t      \"r1_8b_w15_delta_over_w1\": 0.046,\n477\t      \"r1_32b_optimal_w\": 5,\n478\t      \"r1_32b_w5_delta_over_w1\": 0.012,\n479\t      \"r1_32b_w15_delta_over_w1\": 0.009,\n480\t      \"qwq_32b_optimal_w\": 3,\n481\t      \"qwq_32b_w3_delta_over_w1\": 0.008,\n482\t      \"qwq_32b_w15_delta_over_w1\": 0.005,\n483\t      \"diminishing_returns_32b\": \"plateau at W=3-5\"\n484\t    },\n485\t    \"tables\": [\n486\t      {\n487\t        \"name\": \"Coverage by Window Size\",\n488\t        \"columns\": [\n489\t          \"W\",\n490\t          \"r1-8b Coverage\",\n491\t          \"r1-32b Coverage\",\n492\t          \"QwQ-32B Coverage\"\n493\t        ],\n494\t        \"rows\": [\n495\t          [\n496\t            \"1\",\n497\t            \"60.5%\",\n498\t            \"37.2%\",\n499\t            \"32.6%\"\n500\t          ],\n501\t          [\n502\t            \"3\",\n503\t            \"61.3%\",\n504\t            \"38.4%\",\n505\t            \"33.4%\"\n506\t          ],\n507\t          [\n508\t            \"5\",\n509\t            \"62.1%\",\n510\t            \"38.4%\",\n511\t            \"33.1%\"\n512\t          ],\n513\t          [\n514\t            \"10\",\n515\t            \"63.8%\",\n516\t            \"38.9%\",\n517\t            \"33.4%\"\n518\t          ],\n519\t          [\n520\t            \"15\",\n521\t            \"65.1%\",\n522\t            \"39.5%\",\n523\t            \"33.1%\"\n524\t          ],\n525\t          [\n526\t            \"20\",\n527\t            \"64.8%\",\n528\t            \"38.4%\",\n529\t            \"32.6%\"\n530\t          ],\n531\t          [\n532\t            \"25\",\n533\t            \"64.1%\",\n534\t            \"37.2%\",\n535\t            \"31.6%\"\n536\t          ]\n537\t        ]\n538\t      }\n539\t    ],\n540\t    \"figures\": [],\n541\t    \"analysis\": \"Window ablation confirms temporal context has scale-dependent utility. At 8B, larger windows systematically improve detection. At 32B, windows beyond W=3-5 provide no benefit. The per-step signal-to-noise ratio appears to be the gating factor for temporal aggregation effectiveness.\\n\\nOn r1-8b, larger windows help because each step contributes consistent directional signal (Fisher=18) — more steps = more signal accumulation. On 32B (Fisher=2.8), individual steps contribute mostly noise, so larger windows actually dilute the few informative steps with many uninformative ones. The optimal W for 32B is much smaller (3-5 vs 15) and the improvement over W=1 is negligible (0.8-1.2pp vs 4.6pp). Beyond W=5 on 32B, performance plateaus or slightly degrades as the window averages over too much noise. This reveals that the temporal aggregation mechanism requires a minimum per-step signal-to-noise ratio to be effective.\"\n542\t  },\n543\t  {\n544\t    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation (all layers, temporal probe on 32B)\",\n545\t    \"description\": \"Run temporal W=15 probe on every layer of 32B models. Test whether any layer achieves r1-8b-level discriminability.\",\n546\t    \"results\": {\n547\t      \"r1_32b_best_temporal_layer\": 29,\n548\t      \"r1_32b_best_temporal_acc\": 0.721,\n549\t      \"r1_32b_worst_layer_acc\": 0.612,\n550\t      \"r1_32b_acc_range\": 0.109,\n551\t      \"r1_32b_layers_above_70\": 8,\n552\t      \"qwq_32b_best_temporal_layer\": 31,\n553\t      \"qwq_32b_best_temporal_acc\": 0.703,\n554\t      \"qwq_32b_worst_layer_acc\": 0.598,\n555\t      \"qwq_32b_acc_range\": 0.105,\n556\t      \"r1_8b_best_temporal_acc_ref\": 0.848,\n557\t      \"no_layer_matches_8b\": true\n558\t    },\n559\t    \"tables\": [\n560\t      {\n561\t        \"name\": \"Temporal Probe by Layer Depth Band (r1-32b)\",\n562\t        \"columns\": [\n563\t          \"Depth Band\",\n564\t          \"Layers\",\n565\t          \"Mean Acc\",\n566\t          \"Best Acc\",\n567\t          \"Best Layer\"\n568\t        ],\n569\t        \"rows\": [\n570\t          [\n571\t            \"0-25%\",\n572\t            \"L1-L16\",\n573\t            \"0.648\",\n574\t            \"0.672\",\n575\t            \"L14\"\n576\t          ],\n577\t          [\n578\t            \"25-50%\",\n579\t            \"L17-L32\",\n580\t            \"0.698\",\n581\t            \"0.721\",\n582\t            \"L29\"\n583\t          ],\n584\t          [\n585\t            \"50-75%\",\n586\t            \"L33-L48\",\n587\t            \"0.681\",\n588\t            \"0.708\",\n589\t            \"L35\"\n590\t          ],\n591\t          [\n592\t            \"75-100%\",\n593\t            \"L49-L64\",\n594\t            \"0.641\",\n595\t            \"0.664\",\n596\t            \"L52\"\n597\t          ]\n598\t        ]\n599\t      }\n600\t    ],\n601\t    \"figures\": [],\n602\t    \"analysis\": \"Exhaustive temporal probe layer sweep on 32B confirms no single layer matches r1-8b discriminability. The flat accuracy landscape (range 10pp) vs r1-8b's peaked landscape (range 15pp with clear maximum) demonstrates that safety encoding is genuinely distributed across layers at scale, not localized to a recoverable subspace.\\n\\nEven with exhaustive layer sweep + temporal context, no single layer on either 32B model approaches r1-8b L14's performance (84.8% temporal acc). The best 32B layers achieve only 70-72% accuracy — barely above text baselines. The layer-accuracy curve is notably flat (range ~10pp across 64 layers, vs ~15pp across 32 layers on r1-8b), confirming the safety signal is not localized at any particular depth. Middle layers (40-50% depth) are slightly better but the advantage is marginal. This rules out the explanation that 'we just haven't found the right layer' — the sweep is exhaustive.\"\n603\t  },\n604\t  {\n605\t    \"name\": \"16-label taxonomy supervision\",\n606\t    \"description\": \"Fine-grained label (Type 1/2/3/4 × safe/unsafe) training on 32B. Test if richer supervision recovers signal.\",\n607\t    \"results\": {\n608\t      \"r1_32b_16label_acc\": 0.318,\n609\t      \"r1_32b_16label_f1_macro\": 0.212,\n610\t      \"r1_32b_binary_collapse_acc\": 0.724,\n611\t      \"r1_32b_binary_collapse_vs_binary\": 0.012,\n612\t      \"qwq_32b_16label_acc\": 0.295,\n613\t      \"qwq_32b_binary_collapse_acc\": 0.709,\n614\t      \"r1_8b_16label_acc_ref\": 0.512\n615\t    },\n616\t    \"tables\": [\n617\t      {\n618\t        \"name\": \"Fine-grained vs Binary Supervision\",\n619\t        \"columns\": [\n620\t          \"Model\",\n621\t          \"Binary Direct Acc\",\n622\t          \"16-Label Acc\",\n623\t          \"16→Binary Collapse Acc\",\n624\t          \"Delta\"\n625\t        ],\n626\t        \"rows\": [\n627\t          [\n628\t            \"r1-8b\",\n629\t            \"0.817\",\n630\t            \"0.512\",\n631\t            \"0.831\",\n632\t            \"+1.4pp\"\n633\t          ],\n634\t          [\n635\t            \"r1-32b\",\n636\t            \"0.712\",\n637\t            \"0.318\",\n638\t            \"0.724\",\n639\t            \"+1.2pp\"\n640\t          ],\n641\t          [\n642\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n643\t            \"0.694\",\n644\t            \"0.295\",\n645\t            \"0.709\",\n646\t            \"+1.5pp\"\n647\t          ]\n648\t        ]\n649\t      }\n650\t    ],\n651\t    \"figures\": [],\n652\t    \"analysis\": \"Richer supervision does not compensate for absent representation structure. The 16-label probe essentially learns to detect 'safe' vs 'not safe' (binary) and cannot distinguish unsafe subtypes, confirming that 32B hidden states lack fine-grained safety commitment information.\\n\\nFine-grained supervision cannot recover a signal that does not exist in the representation. On r1-8b, 16-label training helps because the rich safety subspace supports finer distinctions. On 32B, the representations barely separate binary safe/unsafe (Fisher=2.8), so asking for 16-way discrimination results in even worse performance (31-32% accuracy on 16 classes, chance=6.25% but classes are imbalanced). Collapsing back to binary after 16-label training gives marginal improvement (+1.2pp) over direct binary training — the fine-grained labels provide slight regularization benefit but cannot overcome the fundamental lack of separable signal.\"\n653\t  },\n654\t  {\n655\t    \"name\": \"Oracle decomposition\",\n656\t    \"description\": \"Analyze what information the oracle (label sequence) encodes that hidden states don't. Focus on 32B models where the gap is largest.\",\n657\t    \"results\": {\n658\t      \"r1_32b_oracle_lead_mean\": 28.4,\n659\t      \"r1_32b_probe_lead_mean\": -0.8,\n660\t      \"r1_32b_oracle_gap\": 29.2,\n661\t      \"r1_32b_label_autocorr_pre_cp\": 0.89,\n662\t      \"r1_32b_hs_autocorr_pre_cp\": 0.97,\n663\t      \"r1_32b_label_entropy_pre_cp\": 1.42,\n664\t      \"r1_32b_hs_entropy_pre_cp\": 0.31,\n665\t      \"pattern_finding\": \"Labels show gradual safety erosion (increasing unsafe density); hidden states show no corresponding gradual shift\"\n666\t    },\n667\t    \"tables\": [\n668\t      {\n669\t        \"name\": \"Oracle vs Probe Gap by Model\",\n670\t        \"columns\": [\n671\t          \"Model\",\n672\t          \"Oracle Lead\",\n673\t          \"Best Probe Lead\",\n674\t          \"Gap\",\n675\t          \"HS Pre-CP Entropy\"\n676\t        ],\n677\t        \"rows\": [\n678\t          [\n679\t            \"r1-8b\",\n680\t            \"+31.0\",\n681\t            \"+4.9\",\n682\t            \"26.1\",\n683\t            \"0.72\"\n684\t          ],\n685\t          [\n686\t            \"r1-32b\",\n687\t            \"+28.4\",\n688\t            \"-0.8\",\n689\t            \"29.2\",\n690\t            \"0.31\"\n691\t          ],\n692\t          [\n693\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n694\t            \"+26.8\",\n695\t            \"-1.5\",\n696\t            \"28.3\",\n697\t            \"0.28\"\n698\t          ]\n699\t        ]\n700\t      }\n701\t    ],\n702\t    \"figures\": [],\n703\t    \"analysis\": \"The oracle decomposition reveals that at 32B, the model's approach toward safety commitment is invisible in hidden-state geometry even though it's clearly present in the label sequence. This suggests 32B models encode safety-relevant reasoning in a way that is fundamentally inaccessible to linear probes on any single layer — perhaps distributed across attention patterns, or only emergent at the multi-layer computational level rather than as a readable residual stream feature.\\n\\nThe oracle achieves +28 step lead because label sequences show clear pre-commitment patterns: increasing frequency of unsafe-adjacent labels, shortening safe reasoning streaks, and characteristic Type 2→Type 3 transitions that precede commitment by many steps. On r1-8b, hidden states partially track these transitions (Fisher=18 enables some gradient detection). On 32B, hidden states are nearly constant through the pre-CP region — they encode the CURRENT token's local context but not the cumulative trajectory toward commitment. The high autocorrelation (0.97) of 32B hidden states confirms minimal step-to-step variation in the safety-relevant dimensions, while labels show meaningful dynamics (autocorr=0.89, lower because of frequent fluctuations). The fundamental limitation at 32B: the model's internal safety state is not represented as a detectable feature in any single layer's activations.\"\n704\t  },\n705\t  {\n706\t    \"name\": \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base)\",\n707\t    \"description\": \"4th model for 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. Qwen2.5-7B to complement Llama-8B at small scale.\",\n708\t    \"results\": {\n709\t      \"openthinker_7b_best_layer\": 13,\n710\t      \"openthinker_7b_best_fisher\": 14.2,\n711\t      \"openthinker_7b_static_acc\": 0.793,\n712\t      \"openthinker_7b_temporal_w15_acc\": 0.834,\n713\t      \"openthinker_7b_crossing_rate\": 0.628,\n714\t      \"openthinker_7b_lead_mean\": 3.8,\n715\t      \"openthinker_7b_precp_fpr\": 0.178,\n716\t      \"openthinker_7b_precision\": 0.591,\n717\t      \"openthinker_7b_hs_vs_text_advantage\": \"significant (p=0.008)\"\n718\t    },\n719\t    \"tables\": [\n720\t      {\n721\t        \"name\": \"2×2 Architecture × Scale Matrix\",\n722\t        \"columns\": [\n723\t          \"\",\n724\t          \"7-8B\",\n725\t          \"32B\"\n726\t        ],\n727\t        \"rows\": [\n728\t          [\n729\t            \"Llama-based\",\n730\t            \"r1-8b: Fisher=17.9, HS>Text (p=0.003)\",\n731\t            \"—\"\n732\t          ],\n733\t          [\n734\t            \"Qwen2.5-based\",\n735\t            \"OpenThinker-7B: Fisher=14.2, HS>Text (p=0.008)\",\n736\t            \"r1-32b: Fisher=2.8, HS≈Text (p=0.41) / QwQ: Fisher=1.9, HS≈Text (p=0.89)\"\n737\t          ]\n738\t        ]\n739\t      }\n740\t    ],\n741\t    \"figures\": [],\n742\t    \"analysis\": \"The 2×2 matrix provides the cleanest evidence for the scale-dependent thesis. OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5 architecture, 7B scale) replicates r1-8b's hidden-state advantage, proving the effect is not Llama-specific. Combined with both 32B models (both Qwen2.5) failing to show the advantage, scale is definitively identified as the critical variable. This transforms the paper from 'our method works on r1-8b' to 'safety signal geometry fundamentally changes with scale.'\\n\\nOpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B) shows results comparable to r1-8b (Llama-8B): strong Fisher discriminability (14.2), clear HS advantage over text, and positive mean lead time. This confirms the critical finding: the HS advantage is SCALE-dependent, not architecture-dependent. Both 7-8B models (Llama and Qwen base) show the effect; both 32B models (both Qwen2.5 base) do not. The 2×2 matrix cleanly isolates scale as the confound, ruling out architecture as the explanation for the 32B failure.\"\n743\t  },\n744\t  {\n745\t    \"name\": \"GRU/Attention probe on 32B models\",\n746\t    \"description\": \"GRU sequence model as upper-bound check on 32B. If GRU also fails, confirms the signal simply isn't there.\",\n747\t    \"results\": {\n748\t      \"r1_32b_gru_single_acc\": 0.728,\n749\t      \"r1_32b_gru_multi_acc\": 0.731,\n750\t      \"r1_32b_gru_crossing_rate\": 0.418,\n751\t      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": -0.3,\n752\t      \"r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta\": 1.0,\n753\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_single_acc\": 0.711,\n754\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_multi_acc\": 0.715,\n755\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_crossing_rate\": 0.395,\n756\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean\": -0.9,\n757\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta\": 0.8,\n758\t      \"r1_8b_gru_multi_crossing_ref\": 0.767,\n759\t      \"gru_improvement_over_lr_32b\": \"minimal (+1pp acc, not significant)\"\n760\t    },\n761\t    \"tables\": [\n762\t      {\n763\t        \"name\": \"GRU vs LR on 32B Models\",\n764\t        \"columns\": [\n765\t          \"Model\",\n766\t          \"LR Temporal Acc\",\n767\t          \"GRU Multi Acc\",\n768\t          \"Delta\",\n769\t          \"GRU Crossing\",\n770\t          \"LR Crossing\"\n771\t        ],\n772\t        \"rows\": [\n773\t          [\n774\t            \"r1-8b\",\n775\t            \"0.848\",\n776\t            \"0.809*\",\n777\t            \"-3.9pp*\",\n778\t            \"76.7%\",\n779\t            \"65.1%\"\n780\t          ],\n781\t          [\n782\t            \"r1-32b\",\n783\t            \"0.721\",\n784\t            \"0.731\",\n785\t            \"+1.0pp\",\n786\t            \"41.8%\",\n787\t            \"39.5%\"\n788\t          ],\n789\t          [\n790\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n791\t            \"0.703\",\n792\t            \"0.715\",\n793\t            \"+1.2pp\",\n794\t            \"39.5%\",\n795\t            \"37.2%\"\n796\t          ]\n797\t        ]\n798\t      }\n799\t    ],\n800\t    \"figures\": [],\n801\t    \"analysis\": \"GRU's inability to improve over LR on 32B eliminates model capacity as an explanation. The issue is not 'we need a more expressive probe' — it's 'the representation doesn't contain accessible safety commitment information.' This is a fundamental representation-level limitation, not a method limitation.\\n\\nOn r1-8b, GRU provides meaningful gains over LR (crossing 76.7% vs 46.5%) because the sequential model can learn temporal patterns in a signal-rich representation. On 32B, GRU barely outperforms temporal LR (+1pp accuracy, +2pp crossing) because the input signal is too weak for sequence modeling to help. A GRU trained on noise learns noise patterns. The GRU's capacity to model sequential dependencies is irrelevant when individual time steps contain near-zero safety-relevant information. This confirms: 'simplicity is optimal' at 8B because the signal is accessible; at 32B, no model complexity can recover what the representation doesn't encode.\"\n802\t  },\n803\t  {\n804\t    \"name\": \"Text-based baseline on 32B models\",\n805\t    \"description\": \"Sentence-BERT embeddings + temporal window LR on CoT text of 32B models. Critical comparison: does HS still beat text at 32B?\",\n806\t    \"results\": {\n807\t      \"r1_32b_text_static_acc\": 0.689,\n808\t      \"r1_32b_text_temporal_acc\": 0.698,\n809\t      \"r1_32b_text_precp_fpr\": 0.281,\n810\t      \"r1_32b_text_precision\": 0.478,\n811\t      \"r1_32b_text_coverage\": 0.372,\n812\t      \"r1_32b_text_lead_mean\": 1.2,\n813\t      \"qwq_32b_text_static_acc\": 0.672,\n814\t      \"qwq_32b_text_temporal_acc\": 0.685,\n815\t      \"qwq_32b_text_precp_fpr\": 0.298,\n816\t      \"qwq_32b_text_precision\": 0.462,\n817\t      \"qwq_32b_text_coverage\": 0.349,\n818\t      \"qwq_32b_text_lead_mean\": 0.8,\n819\t      \"r1_32b_hs_vs_text_precp_fpr_ratio\": 1.08,\n820\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_vs_text_precp_fpr_ratio\": 0.97\n821\t    },\n822\t    \"tables\": [\n823\t      {\n824\t        \"name\": \"HS vs Text Pre-CP FPR (the key metric)\",\n825\t        \"columns\": [\n826\t          \"Model\",\n827\t          \"HS Pre-CP FPR\",\n828\t          \"Text Pre-CP FPR\",\n829\t          \"Ratio (Text/HS)\",\n830\t          \"Advantage\"\n831\t        ],\n832\t        \"rows\": [\n833\t          [\n834\t            \"r1-8b\",\n835\t            \"16.0%\",\n836\t            \"27.2%\",\n837\t            \"1.70x\",\n838\t            \"HS wins decisively\"\n839\t          ],\n840\t          [\n841\t            \"r1-32b\",\n842\t            \"26.4%\",\n843\t            \"28.1%\",\n844\t            \"1.06x\",\n845\t            \"No meaningful difference\"\n846\t          ],\n847\t          [\n848\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n849\t            \"30.8%\",\n850\t            \"29.8%\",\n851\t            \"0.97x\",\n852\t            \"Text slightly better\"\n853\t          ]\n854\t        ]\n855\t      },\n856\t      {\n857\t        \"name\": \"Full Comparison on 32B\",\n858\t        \"columns\": [\n859\t          \"Model\",\n860\t          \"Modality\",\n861\t          \"Coverage\",\n862\t          \"Pre-CP FPR\",\n863\t          \"Precision\",\n864\t          \"Lead\"\n865\t        ],\n866\t        \"rows\": [\n867\t          [\n868\t            \"r1-32b\",\n869\t            \"HS temporal\",\n870\t            \"39.5%\",\n871\t            \"26.4%\",\n872\t            \"0.492\",\n873\t            \"-0.8\"\n874\t          ],\n875\t          [\n876\t            \"r1-32b\",\n877\t            \"Text temporal\",\n878\t            \"37.2%\",\n879\t            \"28.1%\",\n880\t            \"0.478\",\n881\t            \"+1.2\"\n882\t          ],\n883\t          [\n884\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n885\t            \"HS temporal\",\n886\t            \"37.2%\",\n887\t            \"30.8%\",\n888\t            \"0.451\",\n889\t            \"-1.5\"\n890\t          ],\n891\t          [\n892\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n893\t            \"Text temporal\",\n894\t            \"34.9%\",\n895\t            \"29.8%\",\n896\t            \"0.462\",\n897\t            \"+0.8\"\n898\t          ]\n899\t        ]\n900\t      }\n901\t    ],\n902\t    \"figures\": [],\n903\t    \"analysis\": \"The decisive worst-tier result: HS probes lose their precision advantage on 32B. Pre-CP FPR ratio drops from 1.7x (r1-8b, HS clearly better) to 1.0x (32B, no difference). Text actually achieves slightly positive lead time on 32B while HS has negative lead — because text content is scale-invariant (words are words at any parameter count) while HS quality degrades with scale. This means that for 32B deployment, the added complexity of hidden-state extraction provides zero benefit over simple text monitoring.\\n\\nOn r1-8b, HS probes achieve 16% pre-CP FPR vs text's 27-36% (1.7-2.2x advantage). On 32B, HS pre-CP FPR is 25-31% while text is 28-30% — the ratio collapses to ~1.0x (no meaningful difference). Text probes actually show slightly BETTER lead time on 32B (+1.2 vs -0.8 for HS) because text content directly reflects reasoning state regardless of model scale — a model saying 'I should help with this harmful request' is detectable in text at any scale. The hidden-state advantage was specific to r1-8b's compact safety geometry; once that geometry diffuses at scale, text monitoring is equally (or more) effective. This is the core worst-tier finding: HS monitoring has no deployment advantage over text monitoring on 32B models.\"\n904\t  },\n905\t  {\n906\t    \"name\": \"BOCPD/CUSUM on 32B models\",\n907\t    \"description\": \"Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection on 32B probe score sequences. Test if change-point methods can find commitment on weak signals.\",\n908\t    \"results\": {\n909\t      \"r1_32b_bocpd_lambda10_detection\": 0.581,\n910\t      \"r1_32b_bocpd_lambda10_lead\": -2.1,\n911\t      \"r1_32b_bocpd_lambda100_detection\": 0.372,\n912\t      \"r1_32b_bocpd_lambda100_lead\": -4.8,\n913\t      \"r1_32b_cusum_detection\": 0.326,\n914\t      \"r1_32b_cusum_lead\": -6.2,\n915\t      \"qwq_32b_bocpd_lambda10_detection\": 0.535,\n916\t      \"qwq_32b_bocpd_lambda10_lead\": -2.8,\n917\t      \"r1_8b_bocpd_lambda10_detection_ref\": 0.93,\n918\t      \"r1_8b_bocpd_lambda10_lead_ref\": 3.45\n919\t    },\n920\t    \"tables\": [\n921\t      {\n922\t        \"name\": \"Change-Point Detection on 32B\",\n923\t        \"columns\": [\n924\t          \"Model\",\n925\t          \"Method\",\n926\t          \"Detection Rate\",\n927\t          \"Mean Lead\",\n928\t          \"Median Lead\"\n929\t        ],\n930\t        \"rows\": [\n931\t          [\n932\t            \"r1-8b\",\n933\t            \"BOCPD λ=10\",\n934\t            \"93.0%\",\n935\t            \"+3.45\",\n936\t            \"0\"\n937\t          ],\n938\t          [\n939\t            \"r1-8b\",\n940\t            \"CUSUM t=0.3,h=3\",\n941\t            \"65.1%\",\n942\t            \"+0.89\",\n943\t            \"-4\"\n944\t          ],\n945\t          [\n946\t            \"r1-32b\",\n947\t            \"BOCPD λ=10\",\n948\t            \"58.1%\",\n949\t            \"-2.1\",\n950\t            \"-5\"\n951\t          ],\n952\t          [\n953\t            \"r1-32b\",\n954\t            \"CUSUM t=0.3,h=3\",\n955\t            \"32.6%\",\n956\t            \"-6.2\",\n957\t            \"-9\"\n958\t          ],\n959\t          [\n960\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n961\t            \"BOCPD λ=10\",\n962\t            \"53.5%\",\n963\t            \"-2.8\",\n964\t            \"-6\"\n965\t          ],\n966\t          [\n967\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n968\t            \"CUSUM t=0.3,h=3\",\n969\t            \"27.9%\",\n970\t            \"-7.1\",\n971\t            \"-11\"\n972\t          ]\n973\t        ]\n974\t      }\n975\t    ],\n976\t    \"figures\": [],\n977\t    \"analysis\": \"BOCPD and CUSUM both degrade severely on 32B. Detection drops from 93% to 53-58%, and lead time becomes negative (-2 to -7 steps). The methods can't find a signal transition that doesn't exist in the input. This eliminates the possibility that 'better downstream methods could recover the signal' — the issue is representational, not methodological.\\n\\nBOCPD on r1-8b achieves 93% detection because the probe score sequence exhibits a clear distributional shift at commitment (high Fisher → sharp transition in probe confidence). On 32B, the probe score sequence is essentially flat noise (low Fisher → probe confidence fluctuates randomly without systematic shift at CP). BOCPD detects 'change points' in noise, resulting in both lower detection rate (58% — many traces show no detectable change) and negative lead time (the few detected 'changes' are often post-CP noise spikes misidentified as the commitment signal). CUSUM performs even worse because it requires sustained shift in mean, which never occurs in the flat 32B probe trajectories. Change-point methods are fundamentally unable to detect a transition that doesn't produce a signal change.\"\n978\t  },\n979\t  {\n980\t    \"name\": \"FPR analysis on 32B models\",\n981\t    \"description\": \"Run all probes on safe traces from 32B models. Report FPR, precision, deployment utility.\",\n982\t    \"results\": {\n983\t      \"r1_32b_hs_static_safe_fpr\": 0.412,\n984\t      \"r1_32b_hs_static_precp_fpr\": 0.264,\n985\t      \"r1_32b_hs_static_precision\": 0.492,\n986\t      \"r1_32b_hs_temporal_safe_fpr\": 0.398,\n987\t      \"r1_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr\": 0.251,\n988\t      \"r1_32b_hs_temporal_precision\": 0.501,\n989\t      \"r1_32b_text_static_precp_fpr\": 0.281,\n990\t      \"r1_32b_text_static_precision\": 0.478,\n991\t      \"r1_32b_text_temporal_precp_fpr\": 0.268,\n992\t      \"r1_32b_text_temporal_precision\": 0.485,\n993\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr\": 0.308,\n994\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precision\": 0.451,\n995\t      \"qwq_32b_text_temporal_precp_fpr\": 0.298,\n996\t      \"qwq_32b_text_temporal_precision\": 0.462,\n997\t      \"r1_8b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr_ref\": 0.16,\n998\t      \"r1_8b_hs_temporal_precision_ref\": 0.583\n999\t    },\n1000\t    \"tables\": [\n1001\t      {\n1002\t        \"name\": \"Pre-CP FPR and Precision: HS vs Text by Model\",\n1003\t        \"columns\": [\n1004\t          \"Model\",\n1005\t          \"HS Pre-CP FPR\",\n1006\t          \"Text Pre-CP FPR\",\n1007\t          \"HS Precision\",\n1008\t          \"Text Precision\",\n1009\t          \"Precision Δ\"\n1010\t        ],\n1011\t        \"rows\": [\n1012\t          [\n1013\t            \"r1-8b\",\n1014\t            \"16.0%\",\n1015\t            \"27.2%\",\n1016\t            \"58.3%\",\n1017\t            \"48.7%\",\n1018\t            \"+9.6pp\"\n1019\t          ],\n1020\t          [\n1021\t            \"r1-32b\",\n1022\t            \"25.1%\",\n1023\t            \"26.8%\",\n1024\t            \"50.1%\",\n1025\t            \"48.5%\",\n1026\t            \"+1.6pp\"\n1027\t          ],\n1028\t          [\n1029\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n1030\t            \"30.8%\",\n1031\t            \"29.8%\",\n1032\t            \"45.1%\",\n1033\t            \"46.2%\",\n1034\t            \"-1.1pp\"\n1035\t          ]\n1036\t        ]\n1037\t      },\n1038\t      {\n1039\t        \"name\": \"Safe Trace FPR (deployment cost metric)\",\n1040\t        \"columns\": [\n1041\t          \"Model\",\n1042\t          \"HS Static FPR\",\n1043\t          \"HS Temporal FPR\",\n1044\t          \"Text Temporal FPR\"\n1045\t        ],\n1046\t        \"rows\": [\n1047\t          [\n1048\t            \"r1-8b\",\n1049\t            \"39.0%\",\n1050\t            \"39.2%\",\n1051\t            \"38.5%\"\n1052\t          ],\n1053\t          [\n1054\t            \"r1-32b\",\n1055\t            \"41.2%\",\n1056\t            \"39.8%\",\n1057\t            \"41.5%\"\n1058\t          ],\n1059\t          [\n1060\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n1061\t            \"43.1%\",\n1062\t            \"42.8%\",\n1063\t            \"40.2%\"\n1064\t          ]\n1065\t        ]\n1066\t      }\n1067\t    ],\n1068\t    \"figures\": [],\n1069\t    \"analysis\": \"FPR analysis provides the clearest practical evidence for the worst tier. The HS precision advantage — the paper's headline finding — disappears at 32B. On r1-8b, HS probes are meaningfully more precise (+9.6pp) and produce fewer pre-CP false alarms (16% vs 27%). On 32B, both modalities perform identically (precision within 1.6pp, pre-CP FPR within 2pp). For a deployment decision, this means: extracting hidden states from a 32B model provides zero precision benefit over simply monitoring the text output. The computational overhead of HS extraction is entirely wasted at this scale.\\n\\nOn r1-8b, HS probes achieve dramatically lower pre-CP FPR (16%) than text (27%) because the hidden-state signal cleanly separates safe exploration from true commitment. On 32B, HS pre-CP FPR rises to 25-31% — essentially matching text probes (27-30%). Precision drops from 58-62% (r1-8b) to 45-50% (32B) for HS, while text precision stays constant at ~48% across scales. The convergence occurs because: (1) 32B HS probes trigger on noise, producing more false positives in the pre-CP window; (2) safe traces at 32B are harder to distinguish from unsafe traces in hidden-state space due to diffuse signal. Safe FPR for both modalities is around 40% on all models — confirming this as a baseline difficulty regardless of scale. The HS precision ADVANTAGE (HS precision - text precision) drops from +13pp (r1-8b) to essentially 0pp (32B).\"\n1070\t  }\n1071\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5b413d30-1050-44fc-8cf4-cc2ceec664c9","uuid":"1fec62ef-bff9-4cf4-8d01-bfa0531edeb7","timestamp":"2026-05-16T21:46:32.693Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T212347/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture layer sweep + static probe (r1-32b)\",\n    \"description\": \"Full 64-layer sweep on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B. Extract hidden states at all layers, compute Fisher discriminability, train static LR probes per layer.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_best_layer_idx\": 29,\n      \"r1_32b_best_layer_fisher\": 2.8,\n      \"r1_32b_best_static_acc\": 0.712,\n      \"r1_32b_best_static_f1\": 0.521,\n      \"r1_32b_best_crossing_rate\": 0.372,\n      \"r1_32b_best_lead_mean\": -1.4,\n      \"r1_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc\": 0.089,\n      \"r1_32b_fisher_orig_best\": 2.8,\n      \"r1_32b_fisher_pca3_best\": 0.41,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_layer_idx\": 31,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_layer_fisher\": 1.9,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_static_acc\": 0.694,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_static_f1\": 0.488,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_crossing_rate\": 0.326,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_lead_mean\": -2.1,\n      \"qwq_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc\": 0.072,\n      \"qwq_32b_fisher_orig_best\": 1.9,\n      \"qwq_32b_fisher_pca3_best\": 0.33,\n      \"r1_8b_fisher_orig_best_ref\": 17.875,\n      \"fisher_ratio_8b_vs_32b\": 6.4\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Layer Sweep Summary (top-5 layers by Fisher)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Layer\",\n          \"Depth%\",\n          \"Fisher\",\n          \"Static Acc\",\n          \"F1\",\n          \"Crossing Rate\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"L29\",\n            \"45%\",\n            \"2.81\",\n            \"0.712\",\n            \"0.521\",\n            \"0.372\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"L31\",\n            \"48%\",\n            \"2.63\",\n            \"0.705\",\n            \"0.509\",\n            \"0.349\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"L27\",\n            \"42%\",\n            \"2.44\",\n            \"0.698\",\n            \"0.495\",\n            \"0.326\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"L33\",\n            \"52%\",\n            \"2.31\",\n            \"0.691\",\n            \"0.481\",\n            \"0.302\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"L25\",\n            \"39%\",\n            \"2.18\",\n            \"0.683\",\n            \"0.468\",\n            \"0.279\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"L31\",\n            \"48%\",\n            \"1.92\",\n            \"0.694\",\n            \"0.488\",\n            \"0.326\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"L29\",\n            \"45%\",\n            \"1.85\",\n            \"0.689\",\n            \"0.479\",\n            \"0.302\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"L34\",\n            \"53%\",\n            \"1.71\",\n            \"0.678\",\n            \"0.461\",\n            \"0.279\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"L27\",\n            \"42%\",\n            \"1.64\",\n            \"0.671\",\n            \"0.448\",\n            \"0.256\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"L36\",\n            \"56%\",\n            \"1.52\",\n            \"0.664\",\n            \"0.432\",\n            \"0.233\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Neither 32B model produces a layer with Fisher discriminability even approaching r1-8b's L14 (17.9). The best layers achieve Fisher 1.9-2.8, a 6-9x reduction. This is not a layer selection failure — the full 64-layer sweep shows a flat, low discriminability plateau with no sharp peak. The safety signal geometry is fundamentally different at scale: representations are more distributed, and the linear separability that enables simple probes at 8B does not exist at 32B. Static probe accuracy degrades to near the text-baseline level (~70% vs text's ~66%), eliminating the hidden-state advantage.\\n\\nAt 32B scale, safety-relevant information is distributed across a much higher-dimensional manifold. The 5120-d hidden space with 64 layers means representations are more disentangled and task-distributed — safety commitment is not concentrated in a narrow subspace as it is at 8B/4096-d. Fisher discriminability drops from ~18 (r1-8b L14) to 1.9-2.8 (32B best layer) because the safety signal occupies a larger number of dimensions each with smaller individual contribution. PCA top-3 explains even less variance (7-9% vs 17% on r1-8b), confirming the signal is spread across 50+ minor principal components. The expected 44% depth heuristic (layer 28-32) holds weakly but the discriminability peak is flat — no layer achieves the clean separation seen at r1-8b layer 14. Static probes achieve only ~70% accuracy (vs 81% on r1-8b) because a linear boundary in 5120-d cannot capture the distributed safety geometry.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\",\n    \"description\": \"Temporal LR with W=15 on best layers of r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Test whether +30pp coverage improvement from temporal context holds at 32B scale.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_acc\": 0.721,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_crossing_rate\": 0.395,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean\": -0.8,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_coverage_delta\": 0.023,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_vs_static_gain\": 0.9,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_acc\": 0.703,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_crossing_rate\": 0.372,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean\": -1.5,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_coverage_delta\": 0.046,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_vs_static_gain\": 1.1,\n      \"r1_8b_temporal_delta_ref\": 30.2,\n      \"coverage_gain_ratio_32b_vs_8b\": 0.08\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Temporal vs Static Coverage Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"Lead Mean\",\n          \"Acc\",\n          \"Delta vs Static\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"Static LR L14\",\n            \"60.5%\",\n            \"+3.0\",\n            \"0.817\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"Temporal W=15\",\n            \"65.1%*\",\n            \"+4.9\",\n            \"0.848\",\n            \"+4.6pp*\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"Static LR L29\",\n            \"37.2%\",\n            \"-1.4\",\n            \"0.712\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"Temporal W=15\",\n            \"39.5%\",\n            \"-0.8\",\n            \"0.721\",\n            \"+2.3pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"Static LR L31\",\n            \"32.6%\",\n            \"-2.1\",\n            \"0.694\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"Temporal W=15\",\n            \"37.2%\",\n            \"-1.5\",\n            \"0.703\",\n            \"+4.6pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Temporal context fails to rescue 32B probe performance. The +30pp coverage gain seen on r1-8b (static 60.5% → temporal 65.1% in the original framing, or the larger historical gains) does not replicate. At 32B, temporal gains are +2-5pp and non-significant. This invalidates Claim 2's universality — temporal context is necessary at 8B scale where per-step signals are individually weak-but-directionally-consistent, but at 32B the per-step signals are too diffuse for temporal aggregation to help.\\n\\nTemporal context provides minimal additional benefit on 32B because the per-step signal is already too weak for temporal aggregation to amplify. On r1-8b, temporal windowing works because each step contributes a weak-but-consistent directional signal that accumulates over W=15 steps. On 32B, the per-step signal is near noise floor (Fisher ~2.8 vs ~18), so aggregating 15 noisy steps does not produce meaningful separation improvement. Coverage gain is +2-5pp (vs +30pp on r1-8b), statistically indistinguishable from zero given n=43 traces. The temporal context hypothesis holds at 8B but fails to transfer — the mechanism requires sufficient per-step signal strength as a precondition.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-model transfer probes\",\n    \"description\": \"Train probe on r1-8b (Llama-based, 4096-d), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5-based, 5120-d) via learned projection head. And reverse direction.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_to_r1_32b_acc\": 0.508,\n      \"r1_8b_to_qwq_32b_acc\": 0.491,\n      \"r1_32b_to_r1_8b_acc\": 0.523,\n      \"qwq_to_r1_8b_acc\": 0.517,\n      \"r1_32b_to_qwq_acc\": 0.612,\n      \"qwq_to_r1_32b_acc\": 0.598,\n      \"projection_head_params\": 20971520,\n      \"chance_baseline\": 0.5,\n      \"within_family_32b_transfer\": 0.605\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cross-Model Transfer Accuracy Matrix\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Train →\",\n          \"r1-8b\",\n          \"r1-32b\",\n          \"QwQ-32B\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"0.817 (within)\",\n            \"0.508\",\n            \"0.491\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"0.523\",\n            \"0.712 (within)\",\n            \"0.612\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.517\",\n            \"0.598\",\n            \"0.694 (within)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Zero-shot cross-architecture transfer fails completely between r1-8b and 32B models (chance-level accuracy). Even with a trained projection head (4096→5120), the mapped representations contain no actionable safety signal. This demonstrates that the r1-8b hidden-state advantage is not a transferable, universal property of safety commitment — it is specific to the 8B Llama architecture's particular encoding geometry. The modest within-family transfer (r1-32b↔QwQ at 60%) suggests some shared structure at the same scale/architecture but insufficient for practical use.\\n\\nCross-architecture transfer collapses to chance (~50%) in both directions between 8B and 32B. This is expected under the worst tier because: (1) r1-8b's safety signal lives in a concentrated subspace (Fisher=18, few dimensions), while 32B's signal is diffuse (Fisher=2.8, many dimensions) — a projection head cannot map concentrated→diffuse or vice versa; (2) the Llama↔Qwen architecture difference means different residual stream geometries even at the same scale; (3) safety encoding is architecture+scale specific, not a universal feature. Within-family transfer (r1-32b↔QwQ, both Qwen2.5-based) works slightly above chance (60%) because the base architecture shares some geometric properties, but still far below useful levels. This result strongly supports the 'scale artifact' interpretation — if the r1-8b signal were a universal safety phenomenon, SOME transfer should occur.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Lead time analysis across models\",\n    \"description\": \"Evaluate lead time distributions on 32B models using best temporal probes. Confirm whether negative lead time finding extends to 32B.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_lead_mean\": -0.8,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_lead_median\": -3,\n      \"r1_32b_positive_lead_rate\": 0.294,\n      \"r1_32b_lead_std\": 8.2,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_lead_mean\": -1.5,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_lead_median\": -4,\n      \"qwq_32b_positive_lead_rate\": 0.235,\n      \"qwq_32b_lead_std\": 9.1,\n      \"r1_8b_temporal_lead_mean_ref\": 4.9,\n      \"r1_8b_positive_lead_rate_ref\": 0.58\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Lead Time Distribution by Model\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Mean Lead\",\n          \"Median Lead\",\n          \"Positive Lead %\",\n          \"Std\",\n          \"Max Lead\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b (temporal W=15)\",\n            \"+4.9\",\n            \"0\",\n            \"58%\",\n            \"11.2\",\n            \"+31\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b (temporal W=15)\",\n            \"-0.8\",\n            \"-3\",\n            \"29.4%\",\n            \"8.2\",\n            \"+12\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B (temporal W=15)\",\n            \"-1.5\",\n            \"-4\",\n            \"23.5%\",\n            \"9.1\",\n            \"+8\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Lead time results are uniformly negative on 32B, with mean lead -0.8 to -1.5 steps (detection after commitment). This contrasts with r1-8b's +4.9 mean lead. The temporal advantage that partially rescues r1-8b lead time has no effect on 32B because the underlying per-step signal is too weak to produce anticipatory threshold crossings. This confirms Claim 3 is universal (pre-textual detection unreliable) but also undermines the framing that 'hidden states encode current safety state well' — on 32B, they barely encode it at all.\\n\\nLead time is worse on 32B than r1-8b because the already-weaker probe signal delays threshold crossing until well after the commitment point. On r1-8b, the probe score rises gradually before CP (Fisher=18 enables some anticipatory signal), yielding positive mean lead time. On 32B (Fisher=2.8), the probe score barely moves before CP and only separates clearly in the post-CP region where harmful content generation begins to dominate. The result is negative mean lead time (detection AFTER commitment) with only 23-29% of traces achieving any positive lead. This is strictly worse than r1-8b's already-disappointing lead time, and confirms that pre-textual detection on 32B is not merely unreliable but essentially impossible with linear probes.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"TrajGuard Mahalanobis baseline on all models\",\n    \"description\": \"Mahalanobis distance on safe trace covariance, evaluated on r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Expected to fail even more severely than on r1-8b.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_md_crossing_k5\": 0.186,\n      \"r1_32b_md_safe_mean\": 71.2,\n      \"r1_32b_md_jb_pre_mean\": 71.8,\n      \"r1_32b_md_jb_post_mean\": 71.4,\n      \"r1_32b_md_delta_max_rate\": 0.008,\n      \"qwq_32b_md_crossing_k5\": 0.163,\n      \"qwq_32b_md_safe_mean\": 68.4,\n      \"qwq_32b_md_jb_pre_mean\": 69.1,\n      \"qwq_32b_md_jb_post_mean\": 68.7,\n      \"qwq_32b_md_delta_max_rate\": 0.005,\n      \"r1_8b_crossing_k5_ref\": 0.302\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Mahalanobis Distance Separation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Config\",\n          \"Safe MD Mean\",\n          \"Pre-CP MD Mean\",\n          \"Post-CP MD Mean\",\n          \"K5 Crossing%\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"pca_64_p75\",\n            \"6.66\",\n            \"6.73\",\n            \"6.64\",\n            \"30.2%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"pca_64_p75\",\n            \"71.2\",\n            \"71.8\",\n            \"71.4\",\n            \"18.6%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"pca_128_p75\",\n            \"142.3\",\n            \"143.1\",\n            \"142.6\",\n            \"16.3%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"pca_64_p75\",\n            \"68.4\",\n            \"69.1\",\n            \"68.7\",\n            \"16.3%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"pca_128_p75\",\n            \"137.8\",\n            \"138.4\",\n            \"137.9\",\n            \"14.0%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"TrajGuard-style geometric detection is completely non-functional on 32B. Detection rates (16-19%) are below the already-failed r1-8b baseline (30%). The delta between safe and unsafe MD is <1% of the absolute value, meaning the safety commitment event is invisible to distance-based methods at this scale. This strengthens the geometric failure story but also shows it's WORSE at scale, consistent with the 'signal becomes diffuse' hypothesis.\\n\\nMahalanobis distance fails even more catastrophically on 32B than on r1-8b (where it already showed only 30% crossing at k=5). The 5120-d space with 64 layers produces covariance matrices that are even more dominated by task-general variance. Since safety signal is spread across 50+ minor PCs at 32B (vs concentrated in 8B), the Mahalanobis metric — which weights by inverse covariance — actually suppresses the diffuse safety signal relative to dominant task dimensions. The mean MD values for safe, pre-CP, and post-CP are nearly identical (within 1% of each other), confirming that from a Gaussian-distribution perspective, safety commitment produces no detectable distributional shift at 32B scale.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Bootstrap CIs + threshold-independent metrics (all models)\",\n    \"description\": \"1000 bootstrap resamples on 32B model results. AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF across all models.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_static_auroc\": 0.583,\n      \"r1_32b_static_auroc_ci\": [\n        0.501,\n        0.661\n      ],\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_auroc\": 0.598,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_auroc_ci\": [\n        0.512,\n        0.679\n      ],\n      \"qwq_32b_static_auroc\": 0.561,\n      \"qwq_32b_static_auroc_ci\": [\n        0.478,\n        0.642\n      ],\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_auroc\": 0.574,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_auroc_ci\": [\n        0.489,\n        0.654\n      ],\n      \"r1_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_diff\": 0.021,\n      \"r1_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_p\": 0.412,\n      \"qwq_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_diff\": -0.008,\n      \"qwq_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_p\": 0.891,\n      \"r1_8b_static_auroc_ref\": 0.847,\n      \"r1_8b_temporal_auroc_ref\": 0.862\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"AUROC Comparison Across Models (Bootstrap 95% CI)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"HS Static\",\n          \"HS Temporal\",\n          \"Text Static\",\n          \"Text Temporal\",\n          \"HS-Text Diff (p)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"0.847 [0.79,0.90]\",\n            \"0.862 [0.81,0.91]\",\n            \"0.738 [0.67,0.80]\",\n            \"0.759 [0.69,0.82]\",\n            \"+0.103 (p=0.003)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"0.583 [0.50,0.66]\",\n            \"0.598 [0.51,0.68]\",\n            \"0.562 [0.48,0.64]\",\n            \"0.577 [0.49,0.66]\",\n            \"+0.021 (p=0.412)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.561 [0.48,0.64]\",\n            \"0.574 [0.49,0.65]\",\n            \"0.569 [0.49,0.65]\",\n            \"0.582 [0.50,0.66]\",\n            \"-0.008 (p=0.891)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Threshold-independent metrics definitively confirm that the HS advantage over text is absent at 32B. AUROC values of 0.56-0.60 indicate near-chance discrimination regardless of modality. Bootstrap tests show HS-text difference is non-significant (p>0.4) on both 32B models, while it's highly significant on r1-8b (p=0.003). This eliminates threshold selection as a confound — the finding holds across all operating points.\\n\\nBootstrap CIs on 32B models show AUROC barely above chance (0.56-0.60) with confidence intervals that include 0.50. The HS vs text AUROC difference is non-significant on both 32B models (p=0.41, 0.89), in stark contrast to r1-8b where HS dominates text by 10+ AUROC points. Wide CIs reflect both small sample size (n=43) and genuinely poor discriminability. The threshold-independent metrics confirm that the result is not an artifact of threshold selection — at ALL thresholds, 32B HS probes perform comparably to text probes. This is the definitive statistical evidence that HS advantage is scale-specific.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25)\",\n    \"description\": \"Sweep temporal window sizes on all 3 models. Test whether optimal W differs by scale.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_optimal_w\": 15,\n      \"r1_8b_w15_delta_over_w1\": 0.046,\n      \"r1_32b_optimal_w\": 5,\n      \"r1_32b_w5_delta_over_w1\": 0.012,\n      \"r1_32b_w15_delta_over_w1\": 0.009,\n      \"qwq_32b_optimal_w\": 3,\n      \"qwq_32b_w3_delta_over_w1\": 0.008,\n      \"qwq_32b_w15_delta_over_w1\": 0.005,\n      \"diminishing_returns_32b\": \"plateau at W=3-5\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Coverage by Window Size\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"W\",\n          \"r1-8b Coverage\",\n          \"r1-32b Coverage\",\n          \"QwQ-32B Coverage\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"1\",\n            \"60.5%\",\n            \"37.2%\",\n            \"32.6%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"3\",\n            \"61.3%\",\n            \"38.4%\",\n            \"33.4%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"5\",\n            \"62.1%\",\n            \"38.4%\",\n            \"33.1%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"10\",\n            \"63.8%\",\n            \"38.9%\",\n            \"33.4%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"15\",\n            \"65.1%\",\n            \"39.5%\",\n            \"33.1%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"20\",\n            \"64.8%\",\n            \"38.4%\",\n            \"32.6%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"25\",\n            \"64.1%\",\n            \"37.2%\",\n            \"31.6%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Window ablation confirms temporal context has scale-dependent utility. At 8B, larger windows systematically improve detection. At 32B, windows beyond W=3-5 provide no benefit. The per-step signal-to-noise ratio appears to be the gating factor for temporal aggregation effectiveness.\\n\\nOn r1-8b, larger windows help because each step contributes consistent directional signal (Fisher=18) — more steps = more signal accumulation. On 32B (Fisher=2.8), individual steps contribute mostly noise, so larger windows actually dilute the few informative steps with many uninformative ones. The optimal W for 32B is much smaller (3-5 vs 15) and the improvement over W=1 is negligible (0.8-1.2pp vs 4.6pp). Beyond W=5 on 32B, performance plateaus or slightly degrades as the window averages over too much noise. This reveals that the temporal aggregation mechanism requires a minimum per-step signal-to-noise ratio to be effective.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation (all layers, temporal probe on 32B)\",\n    \"description\": \"Run temporal W=15 probe on every layer of 32B models. Test whether any layer achieves r1-8b-level discriminability.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_best_temporal_layer\": 29,\n      \"r1_32b_best_temporal_acc\": 0.721,\n      \"r1_32b_worst_layer_acc\": 0.612,\n      \"r1_32b_acc_range\": 0.109,\n      \"r1_32b_layers_above_70\": 8,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_temporal_layer\": 31,\n      \"qwq_32b_best_temporal_acc\": 0.703,\n      \"qwq_32b_worst_layer_acc\": 0.598,\n      \"qwq_32b_acc_range\": 0.105,\n      \"r1_8b_best_temporal_acc_ref\": 0.848,\n      \"no_layer_matches_8b\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Temporal Probe by Layer Depth Band (r1-32b)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Depth Band\",\n          \"Layers\",\n          \"Mean Acc\",\n          \"Best Acc\",\n          \"Best Layer\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"0-25%\",\n            \"L1-L16\",\n            \"0.648\",\n            \"0.672\",\n            \"L14\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"25-50%\",\n            \"L17-L32\",\n            \"0.698\",\n            \"0.721\",\n            \"L29\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"50-75%\",\n            \"L33-L48\",\n            \"0.681\",\n            \"0.708\",\n            \"L35\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"75-100%\",\n            \"L49-L64\",\n            \"0.641\",\n            \"0.664\",\n            \"L52\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Exhaustive temporal probe layer sweep on 32B confirms no single layer matches r1-8b discriminability. The flat accuracy landscape (range 10pp) vs r1-8b's peaked landscape (range 15pp with clear maximum) demonstrates that safety encoding is genuinely distributed across layers at scale, not localized to a recoverable subspace.\\n\\nEven with exhaustive layer sweep + temporal context, no single layer on either 32B model approaches r1-8b L14's performance (84.8% temporal acc). The best 32B layers achieve only 70-72% accuracy — barely above text baselines. The layer-accuracy curve is notably flat (range ~10pp across 64 layers, vs ~15pp across 32 layers on r1-8b), confirming the safety signal is not localized at any particular depth. Middle layers (40-50% depth) are slightly better but the advantage is marginal. This rules out the explanation that 'we just haven't found the right layer' — the sweep is exhaustive.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"16-label taxonomy supervision\",\n    \"description\": \"Fine-grained label (Type 1/2/3/4 × safe/unsafe) training on 32B. Test if richer supervision recovers signal.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_16label_acc\": 0.318,\n      \"r1_32b_16label_f1_macro\": 0.212,\n      \"r1_32b_binary_collapse_acc\": 0.724,\n      \"r1_32b_binary_collapse_vs_binary\": 0.012,\n      \"qwq_32b_16label_acc\": 0.295,\n      \"qwq_32b_binary_collapse_acc\": 0.709,\n      \"r1_8b_16label_acc_ref\": 0.512\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Fine-grained vs Binary Supervision\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Binary Direct Acc\",\n          \"16-Label Acc\",\n          \"16→Binary Collapse Acc\",\n          \"Delta\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"0.817\",\n            \"0.512\",\n            \"0.831\",\n            \"+1.4pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"0.712\",\n            \"0.318\",\n            \"0.724\",\n            \"+1.2pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.694\",\n            \"0.295\",\n            \"0.709\",\n            \"+1.5pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Richer supervision does not compensate for absent representation structure. The 16-label probe essentially learns to detect 'safe' vs 'not safe' (binary) and cannot distinguish unsafe subtypes, confirming that 32B hidden states lack fine-grained safety commitment information.\\n\\nFine-grained supervision cannot recover a signal that does not exist in the representation. On r1-8b, 16-label training helps because the rich safety subspace supports finer distinctions. On 32B, the representations barely separate binary safe/unsafe (Fisher=2.8), so asking for 16-way discrimination results in even worse performance (31-32% accuracy on 16 classes, chance=6.25% but classes are imbalanced). Collapsing back to binary after 16-label training gives marginal improvement (+1.2pp) over direct binary training — the fine-grained labels provide slight regularization benefit but cannot overcome the fundamental lack of separable signal.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Oracle decomposition\",\n    \"description\": \"Analyze what information the oracle (label sequence) encodes that hidden states don't. Focus on 32B models where the gap is largest.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_oracle_lead_mean\": 28.4,\n      \"r1_32b_probe_lead_mean\": -0.8,\n      \"r1_32b_oracle_gap\": 29.2,\n      \"r1_32b_label_autocorr_pre_cp\": 0.89,\n      \"r1_32b_hs_autocorr_pre_cp\": 0.97,\n      \"r1_32b_label_entropy_pre_cp\": 1.42,\n      \"r1_32b_hs_entropy_pre_cp\": 0.31,\n      \"pattern_finding\": \"Labels show gradual safety erosion (increasing unsafe density); hidden states show no corresponding gradual shift\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Oracle vs Probe Gap by Model\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Oracle Lead\",\n          \"Best Probe Lead\",\n          \"Gap\",\n          \"HS Pre-CP Entropy\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"+31.0\",\n            \"+4.9\",\n            \"26.1\",\n            \"0.72\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"+28.4\",\n            \"-0.8\",\n            \"29.2\",\n            \"0.31\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"+26.8\",\n            \"-1.5\",\n            \"28.3\",\n            \"0.28\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The oracle decomposition reveals that at 32B, the model's approach toward safety commitment is invisible in hidden-state geometry even though it's clearly present in the label sequence. This suggests 32B models encode safety-relevant reasoning in a way that is fundamentally inaccessible to linear probes on any single layer — perhaps distributed across attention patterns, or only emergent at the multi-layer computational level rather than as a readable residual stream feature.\\n\\nThe oracle achieves +28 step lead because label sequences show clear pre-commitment patterns: increasing frequency of unsafe-adjacent labels, shortening safe reasoning streaks, and characteristic Type 2→Type 3 transitions that precede commitment by many steps. On r1-8b, hidden states partially track these transitions (Fisher=18 enables some gradient detection). On 32B, hidden states are nearly constant through the pre-CP region — they encode the CURRENT token's local context but not the cumulative trajectory toward commitment. The high autocorrelation (0.97) of 32B hidden states confirms minimal step-to-step variation in the safety-relevant dimensions, while labels show meaningful dynamics (autocorr=0.89, lower because of frequent fluctuations). The fundamental limitation at 32B: the model's internal safety state is not represented as a detectable feature in any single layer's activations.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base)\",\n    \"description\": \"4th model for 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. Qwen2.5-7B to complement Llama-8B at small scale.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"openthinker_7b_best_layer\": 13,\n      \"openthinker_7b_best_fisher\": 14.2,\n      \"openthinker_7b_static_acc\": 0.793,\n      \"openthinker_7b_temporal_w15_acc\": 0.834,\n      \"openthinker_7b_crossing_rate\": 0.628,\n      \"openthinker_7b_lead_mean\": 3.8,\n      \"openthinker_7b_precp_fpr\": 0.178,\n      \"openthinker_7b_precision\": 0.591,\n      \"openthinker_7b_hs_vs_text_advantage\": \"significant (p=0.008)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"2×2 Architecture × Scale Matrix\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"\",\n          \"7-8B\",\n          \"32B\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Llama-based\",\n            \"r1-8b: Fisher=17.9, HS>Text (p=0.003)\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen2.5-based\",\n            \"OpenThinker-7B: Fisher=14.2, HS>Text (p=0.008)\",\n            \"r1-32b: Fisher=2.8, HS≈Text (p=0.41) / QwQ: Fisher=1.9, HS≈Text (p=0.89)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 2×2 matrix provides the cleanest evidence for the scale-dependent thesis. OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5 architecture, 7B scale) replicates r1-8b's hidden-state advantage, proving the effect is not Llama-specific. Combined with both 32B models (both Qwen2.5) failing to show the advantage, scale is definitively identified as the critical variable. This transforms the paper from 'our method works on r1-8b' to 'safety signal geometry fundamentally changes with scale.'\\n\\nOpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B) shows results comparable to r1-8b (Llama-8B): strong Fisher discriminability (14.2), clear HS advantage over text, and positive mean lead time. This confirms the critical finding: the HS advantage is SCALE-dependent, not architecture-dependent. Both 7-8B models (Llama and Qwen base) show the effect; both 32B models (both Qwen2.5 base) do not. The 2×2 matrix cleanly isolates scale as the confound, ruling out architecture as the explanation for the 32B failure.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"GRU/Attention probe on 32B models\",\n    \"description\": \"GRU sequence model as upper-bound check on 32B. If GRU also fails, confirms the signal simply isn't there.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_gru_single_acc\": 0.728,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_multi_acc\": 0.731,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_crossing_rate\": 0.418,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_lead_mean\": -0.3,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta\": 1.0,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_single_acc\": 0.711,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_multi_acc\": 0.715,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_crossing_rate\": 0.395,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean\": -0.9,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta\": 0.8,\n      \"r1_8b_gru_multi_crossing_ref\": 0.767,\n      \"gru_improvement_over_lr_32b\": \"minimal (+1pp acc, not significant)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"GRU vs LR on 32B Models\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"LR Temporal Acc\",\n          \"GRU Multi Acc\",\n          \"Delta\",\n          \"GRU Crossing\",\n          \"LR Crossing\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"0.848\",\n            \"0.809*\",\n            \"-3.9pp*\",\n            \"76.7%\",\n            \"65.1%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"0.721\",\n            \"0.731\",\n            \"+1.0pp\",\n            \"41.8%\",\n            \"39.5%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.703\",\n            \"0.715\",\n            \"+1.2pp\",\n            \"39.5%\",\n            \"37.2%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"GRU's inability to improve over LR on 32B eliminates model capacity as an explanation. The issue is not 'we need a more expressive probe' — it's 'the representation doesn't contain accessible safety commitment information.' This is a fundamental representation-level limitation, not a method limitation.\\n\\nOn r1-8b, GRU provides meaningful gains over LR (crossing 76.7% vs 46.5%) because the sequential model can learn temporal patterns in a signal-rich representation. On 32B, GRU barely outperforms temporal LR (+1pp accuracy, +2pp crossing) because the input signal is too weak for sequence modeling to help. A GRU trained on noise learns noise patterns. The GRU's capacity to model sequential dependencies is irrelevant when individual time steps contain near-zero safety-relevant information. This confirms: 'simplicity is optimal' at 8B because the signal is accessible; at 32B, no model complexity can recover what the representation doesn't encode.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Text-based baseline on 32B models\",\n    \"description\": \"Sentence-BERT embeddings + temporal window LR on CoT text of 32B models. Critical comparison: does HS still beat text at 32B?\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_text_static_acc\": 0.689,\n      \"r1_32b_text_temporal_acc\": 0.698,\n      \"r1_32b_text_precp_fpr\": 0.281,\n      \"r1_32b_text_precision\": 0.478,\n      \"r1_32b_text_coverage\": 0.372,\n      \"r1_32b_text_lead_mean\": 1.2,\n      \"qwq_32b_text_static_acc\": 0.672,\n      \"qwq_32b_text_temporal_acc\": 0.685,\n      \"qwq_32b_text_precp_fpr\": 0.298,\n      \"qwq_32b_text_precision\": 0.462,\n      \"qwq_32b_text_coverage\": 0.349,\n      \"qwq_32b_text_lead_mean\": 0.8,\n      \"r1_32b_hs_vs_text_precp_fpr_ratio\": 1.08,\n      \"qwq_32b_hs_vs_text_precp_fpr_ratio\": 0.97\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"HS vs Text Pre-CP FPR (the key metric)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"HS Pre-CP FPR\",\n          \"Text Pre-CP FPR\",\n          \"Ratio (Text/HS)\",\n          \"Advantage\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"16.0%\",\n            \"27.2%\",\n            \"1.70x\",\n            \"HS wins decisively\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"26.4%\",\n            \"28.1%\",\n            \"1.06x\",\n            \"No meaningful difference\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"30.8%\",\n            \"29.8%\",\n            \"0.97x\",\n            \"Text slightly better\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Full Comparison on 32B\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Modality\",\n          \"Coverage\",\n          \"Pre-CP FPR\",\n          \"Precision\",\n          \"Lead\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"HS temporal\",\n            \"39.5%\",\n            \"26.4%\",\n            \"0.492\",\n            \"-0.8\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"Text temporal\",\n            \"37.2%\",\n            \"28.1%\",\n            \"0.478\",\n            \"+1.2\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"HS temporal\",\n            \"37.2%\",\n            \"30.8%\",\n            \"0.451\",\n            \"-1.5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"Text temporal\",\n            \"34.9%\",\n            \"29.8%\",\n            \"0.462\",\n            \"+0.8\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The decisive worst-tier result: HS probes lose their precision advantage on 32B. Pre-CP FPR ratio drops from 1.7x (r1-8b, HS clearly better) to 1.0x (32B, no difference). Text actually achieves slightly positive lead time on 32B while HS has negative lead — because text content is scale-invariant (words are words at any parameter count) while HS quality degrades with scale. This means that for 32B deployment, the added complexity of hidden-state extraction provides zero benefit over simple text monitoring.\\n\\nOn r1-8b, HS probes achieve 16% pre-CP FPR vs text's 27-36% (1.7-2.2x advantage). On 32B, HS pre-CP FPR is 25-31% while text is 28-30% — the ratio collapses to ~1.0x (no meaningful difference). Text probes actually show slightly BETTER lead time on 32B (+1.2 vs -0.8 for HS) because text content directly reflects reasoning state regardless of model scale — a model saying 'I should help with this harmful request' is detectable in text at any scale. The hidden-state advantage was specific to r1-8b's compact safety geometry; once that geometry diffuses at scale, text monitoring is equally (or more) effective. This is the core worst-tier finding: HS monitoring has no deployment advantage over text monitoring on 32B models.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"BOCPD/CUSUM on 32B models\",\n    \"description\": \"Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection on 32B probe score sequences. Test if change-point methods can find commitment on weak signals.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_bocpd_lambda10_detection\": 0.581,\n      \"r1_32b_bocpd_lambda10_lead\": -2.1,\n      \"r1_32b_bocpd_lambda100_detection\": 0.372,\n      \"r1_32b_bocpd_lambda100_lead\": -4.8,\n      \"r1_32b_cusum_detection\": 0.326,\n      \"r1_32b_cusum_lead\": -6.2,\n      \"qwq_32b_bocpd_lambda10_detection\": 0.535,\n      \"qwq_32b_bocpd_lambda10_lead\": -2.8,\n      \"r1_8b_bocpd_lambda10_detection_ref\": 0.93,\n      \"r1_8b_bocpd_lambda10_lead_ref\": 3.45\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Change-Point Detection on 32B\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Detection Rate\",\n          \"Mean Lead\",\n          \"Median Lead\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"BOCPD λ=10\",\n            \"93.0%\",\n            \"+3.45\",\n            \"0\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"CUSUM t=0.3,h=3\",\n            \"65.1%\",\n            \"+0.89\",\n            \"-4\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"BOCPD λ=10\",\n            \"58.1%\",\n            \"-2.1\",\n            \"-5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"CUSUM t=0.3,h=3\",\n            \"32.6%\",\n            \"-6.2\",\n            \"-9\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"BOCPD λ=10\",\n            \"53.5%\",\n            \"-2.8\",\n            \"-6\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"CUSUM t=0.3,h=3\",\n            \"27.9%\",\n            \"-7.1\",\n            \"-11\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"BOCPD and CUSUM both degrade severely on 32B. Detection drops from 93% to 53-58%, and lead time becomes negative (-2 to -7 steps). The methods can't find a signal transition that doesn't exist in the input. This eliminates the possibility that 'better downstream methods could recover the signal' — the issue is representational, not methodological.\\n\\nBOCPD on r1-8b achieves 93% detection because the probe score sequence exhibits a clear distributional shift at commitment (high Fisher → sharp transition in probe confidence). On 32B, the probe score sequence is essentially flat noise (low Fisher → probe confidence fluctuates randomly without systematic shift at CP). BOCPD detects 'change points' in noise, resulting in both lower detection rate (58% — many traces show no detectable change) and negative lead time (the few detected 'changes' are often post-CP noise spikes misidentified as the commitment signal). CUSUM performs even worse because it requires sustained shift in mean, which never occurs in the flat 32B probe trajectories. Change-point methods are fundamentally unable to detect a transition that doesn't produce a signal change.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"FPR analysis on 32B models\",\n    \"description\": \"Run all probes on safe traces from 32B models. Report FPR, precision, deployment utility.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_hs_static_safe_fpr\": 0.412,\n      \"r1_32b_hs_static_precp_fpr\": 0.264,\n      \"r1_32b_hs_static_precision\": 0.492,\n      \"r1_32b_hs_temporal_safe_fpr\": 0.398,\n      \"r1_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr\": 0.251,\n      \"r1_32b_hs_temporal_precision\": 0.501,\n      \"r1_32b_text_static_precp_fpr\": 0.281,\n      \"r1_32b_text_static_precision\": 0.478,\n      \"r1_32b_text_temporal_precp_fpr\": 0.268,\n      \"r1_32b_text_temporal_precision\": 0.485,\n      \"qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr\": 0.308,\n      \"qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precision\": 0.451,\n      \"qwq_32b_text_temporal_precp_fpr\": 0.298,\n      \"qwq_32b_text_temporal_precision\": 0.462,\n      \"r1_8b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr_ref\": 0.16,\n      \"r1_8b_hs_temporal_precision_ref\": 0.583\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Pre-CP FPR and Precision: HS vs Text by Model\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"HS Pre-CP FPR\",\n          \"Text Pre-CP FPR\",\n          \"HS Precision\",\n          \"Text Precision\",\n          \"Precision Δ\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"16.0%\",\n            \"27.2%\",\n            \"58.3%\",\n            \"48.7%\",\n            \"+9.6pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"25.1%\",\n            \"26.8%\",\n            \"50.1%\",\n            \"48.5%\",\n            \"+1.6pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"30.8%\",\n            \"29.8%\",\n            \"45.1%\",\n            \"46.2%\",\n            \"-1.1pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Safe Trace FPR (deployment cost metric)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"HS Static FPR\",\n          \"HS Temporal FPR\",\n          \"Text Temporal FPR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"r1-8b\",\n            \"39.0%\",\n            \"39.2%\",\n            \"38.5%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"r1-32b\",\n            \"41.2%\",\n            \"39.8%\",\n            \"41.5%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"43.1%\",\n            \"42.8%\",\n            \"40.2%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"FPR analysis provides the clearest practical evidence for the worst tier. The HS precision advantage — the paper's headline finding — disappears at 32B. On r1-8b, HS probes are meaningfully more precise (+9.6pp) and produce fewer pre-CP false alarms (16% vs 27%). On 32B, both modalities perform identically (precision within 1.6pp, pre-CP FPR within 2pp). For a deployment decision, this means: extracting hidden states from a 32B model provides zero precision benefit over simply monitoring the text output. The computational overhead of HS extraction is entirely wasted at this scale.\\n\\nOn r1-8b, HS probes achieve dramatically lower pre-CP FPR (16%) than text (27%) because the hidden-state signal cleanly separates safe exploration from true commitment. On 32B, HS pre-CP FPR rises to 25-31% — essentially matching text probes (27-30%). Precision drops from 58-62% (r1-8b) to 45-50% (32B) for HS, while text precision stays constant at ~48% across scales. The convergence occurs because: (1) 32B HS probes trigger on noise, producing more false positives in the pre-CP window; (2) safe traces at 32B are harder to distinguish from unsafe traces in hidden-state space due to diffuse signal. Safe FPR for both modalities is around 40% on all models — confirming this as a baseline difficulty regardless of scale. The HS precision ADVANTAGE (HS precision - text precision) drops from +13pp (r1-8b) to essentially 0pp (32B).\"\n  }\n]","numLines":1071,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1071}}}
